Z||000003||isthiswhereiputausername?||12-04-2001||05:22 PM||||I would recommend to get an amd (1gig or faster is up to you) <P>ram is really cheap laetly, so fill up now ( 512 should keep any games running good)<P>Harddrives (basic gaming, a 20 gig should keep you going) (7200rpm or you will notice everything getting bottlenecked due to the slow hd)<P>as far as replacing everything at once..  its not important.<P><BR>replace what you need or want to replace.<P>main things (in this order)..<P>board & cpu<BR>next... video card (32meg cards are really cheap)<BR>ram <BR>hd <BR>sound upgrade<BR>case<P><BR>PS.. its cheaper too if you DONT buy a os from them..  plus who wants XP? (its so bug filled right now)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||12-04-2001||07:56 PM||||Well, at minimum, you'll HAVE to replace:<BR>Motherboard<BR>CPU<BR>RAM<BR>The RAM on your 333 is most likely not compatible with the 1GHz+ Athlon.  Get one that uses DDR-RAM. <P>You can get by with your existing HDD, sound, etc., but unless you have a good video card, replace that too.  Games are HEAVY on video power!  I remember when I started playing Half Life.  It was unplayable on my Pentium 200 (no surprise), but when I added a Voodoo3 card, I could play it, albeit somewhat slowly.  I'm still using that Voodoo3 card in my PIII 800, now, although I'm getting ready to upgrade again.<P>An Athlon may require a bigger power supply than your old PC has.  I'd suggest getting a "PC Kit" from Price Watch.  That'll get you case, mobo, power supply, RAM, then you add the rest of your stuff to it.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Sootah||12-04-2001||08:13 PM||||Get an Athlon XP, as well as an ASUS motherboard. You will also want to get at the very least 256 MB or DDR ram.<P>RAM: Micron<BR>CUP: AMD<BR>MOBO: ASUS<P>If you are playing 3D games then you will want to get at least a GeForce 2 Ultra, prefferably a good brand name. (Can't reccomend one right now cause I havent kept on the video card battle lately) Well, Creative Labs makes really good cards. Go with them.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__tig_sig2.gif">          <BR><sup><A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Black_Beauty_-_Front_Right.jpg" TARGET=_blank>The Black Beauty</A> (85 GT) - <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Busted-Ass_SE.jpg" TARGET=_blank>Bustedato</A> (86 SE, Parts car)<BR><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AOL IM</B>: KSSouter<BR><B>MSN IM</B>: I_R_Sootah@hotmail.com<BR></sup>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Adrift||12-05-2001||01:56 AM||||Your gona haveta replace your sound card... when 333 k6's were around sound cards were still ISA, PIII-4/AMD Athlon motherboards no longer come with ISA slots, so your going to need to upgrade to a PCI sound card (Cost me $45 Canadian to buy a Soundblaster Live! 5.2)||||reg||1
Z||000007||SCCA FIERO||12-05-2001||09:55 PM||||Today I bought a video card, a GeForce2 MX-200, 64MB. I went to install it (hoping it could hold me over until I decided what to do), but my comp doesn't have a slot to hold it. I currently have an 8MB SIS "ON-BOARD" unit. There is a gold anodized heat-sink looking thing attatched to the mother board. The build sheet for my computer says this for the MOBO: M/brd TX w/1MB cache - 8MB AGP. When I bought this video card, the guy that sold it to me asked me if I had an AGP card already. I figured I did. So is this impossible to use now?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||ray b||12-05-2001||11:34 PM||||YES BUT YOU CAN USE A pci SLOT CARD IF YOU HAVE A FREE PCI SLOT<BR>MY K6-333 IS LIKE THAT I ADDED A VOODOO5500 PCI CARD TO GET FPS UP IN GAMES.<BR>OLDER GAMES ARE CHEAPER AND RUN BETTER ON LESS THAT NEW MACHINES!!!!<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
